- [x] Describe every call in OpenAPI, and test that none is missing
  Today `GET /api/openapi.json` describes 7 of the 19 calls (`photo`, `pronunciation`, `file`, `fetched`, `person-at`, `search`, `compose`). I'd add the other 12 to `spec()` in `internal/db/openapi.go`, with request and response schemas read off each handler's struct. For example, `unsubscribe` would take `{"group": "grp…", "subscribed": false}` and answer `{"result": ["grp…"]}`. I'd also add a test that mounts every registration (`Register`, `RegisterSearch`, `RegisterDrafts`, `RegisterCompose`, `RegisterInvitations`, `RegisterTriage`) and fails on any `/api/do/` route that `spec()` doesn't describe. Adding a call later without its spec entry then turns the suite red.
- [x] Drop `/api/do/guest`; Loop adds guests the way Celebrate already does
  There are two ways to turn an address into a guest. **Celebrate** (`web/celebrate/edit.js`) calls `person-at`, then inserts the guest's `PERSON` and `PERSON_EMAIL` rows in the same batch as the ticket. **Loop** (`web/loop/pages/group.js` `addGuests`) calls `/api/do/guest` once per guest, which runs the same `personAt` lookup and inserts the same two rows, and then writes the `MEMBER` rows in a second batch.
  Adding the guest sam@example.org to a list, today in Loop:
  ```
  POST /api/do/guest {"email":"sam@example.org","name":"Sam"} → {"result":["per_new"]}   (guest written now)
  POST /api/q {"batch":[{"insert":"MEMBER","row":{"group":"grp…","person":"per_new","member":"yes"}}]}
  ```
  After the change:
  ```
  POST /api/do/person-at {"address":"sam@example.org"} → {"person":""}
  POST /api/q {"batch":[{"insert":"PERSON","as":"g","row":{"source":"guest","name_long_override":"Sam"}},
                        {"insert":"PERSON_EMAIL","row":{"person":"@g","address":"sam@example.org","primary":true,"source":"guest"}},
                        {"insert":"MEMBER","row":{"group":"grp…","person":"@g","member":"yes"}}]}
  ```
  One path instead of two, and `guest.go` goes away. A failed membership write no longer leaves an orphan guest behind. People see no difference.
- [x] Make compose's docs match who can actually call it
  `docs/admin/admin.md` and the OpenAPI description both say `compose` runs "on the admin host alone". In fact it is on every host, like every `/api/` route, and anyone in the directory can call it, within their hourly Claude allowance. It only writes a query, never runs one, so I'd change the wording to "anyone in the directory, counted against their hourly Claude allowance" rather than add an admin check.
- [x] Rename `unsubscribe` to `subscription`
  `POST /api/do/unsubscribe {"group":"grp…","subscribed":true}` re-subscribes, which is what Loop's toggle sends when someone turns a list back on (`web/loop/pages/group.js` line 807). It would become `POST /api/do/subscription` with the same body. I'd also have it answer 403 instead of 400 when there's no person behind the call (today it says "only a person unsubscribes" with a 400). Loop's page is the only caller.
- [x] Test the calls nothing tests today
  The handler checks for `person-at`, `unsubscribe` (not in the group → 404), `describe-charity` (not on the Birthdays team → 403), `describe-activity`, `compose` (no words → 400), `report-draft` and `file-report` (not a super admin → 403, already filed → 409) and `fetched` (not a linked document still to fetch → 400) run in no test. The Claude and GitHub calls would only be tested up to the point where they'd be called, since the tests never reach Claude or GitHub.
- [x] Drop the always-blank `system` fields
  `caller()` never sets `Env.System`, so the `"system", env.System` log fields in `photo`, `pronunciation`, `file`, `fetched` and `compose` always log `system=""`. `compose`'s rate-limit key `env.Viewer+env.System` is just the viewer. I'd remove the fields and key the limit on `env.Viewer`. Nothing anyone sees changes.
- [ ] Read every JSON body with `serve.Decode`
  `wiki` reads its body with `serve.Decode`. The other 13 JSON calls each repeat `json.NewDecoder(http.MaxBytesReader(w, r.Body, queryLimit))` with their own error text. A malformed body sent to `search` answers 400 `send {"words": "…", "limits": {"DOCUMENT": 10}}: invalid character…`; through `serve.Decode` it would answer 400 `bad request body` (or 413 when too large). This is consistent but removes the hints, so I'm not recommending it.

